description:
addressbook.rs:172-181 (create), 231-239 (update), 296-304 (destroy), and the parallel sites in card.rs 144-153, 203-212, 252-261, 461-469. Every backend-error match has the shape:

    Err(BackendSetError::SetError(set_err)) => { ... }
    Err(BackendSetError::Other(e)) => { ... }
    Err(_) => {
        not_created.insert(create_id, json!({
            "type": "serverFail",
            "description": "unhandled backend error variant",
        }));
    }

BackendSetError is defined in jmap-server. If BackendSetError is #[non_exhaustive], then this wildcard arm is correct — future variant additions in jmap-server fall here. If BackendSetError is NOT #[non_exhaustive], the wildcard arm is statically unreachable: the two named variants exhaust the enum.

From existing finding .23's investigation it's noted that some workspace error types lack #[non_exhaustive]. The audit gap: this wildcard either silently masks future-variant breakage (if BackendSetError IS #[non_exhaustive], then the description "unhandled backend error variant" is honest, but contracts the inability to surface a structured error code) or it's dead code (if BackendSetError is NOT #[non_exhaustive]).

Idiomatic Rust answer in either case:
  - If non_exhaustive, the wildcard arm MUST produce something that round-trips informative info — at minimum log the variant via Debug. The current json! body discards the actual error.
  - If not non_exhaustive, delete the wildcard arm.

Same wildcard pattern in canonical jmap-mail-server — propagation needed. Verify the upstream non_exhaustive status with: rg "#\\[non_exhaustive\\]" crate-jmap-server/src/ on the BackendSetError enum.

The duplication across 6 sites in this crate alone is the deeper finding: the BackendSetError → JSON conversion is open-coded six times. A helper like `fn backend_set_err_to_json(e: BackendSetError<...>) -> Value` would centralize it and let the wildcard-arm decision live in one place.
